7
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:23.409
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Binding means linking two or more things together in a way that can be verified. When something is bound, you can mathematically prove that the pieces belong together and have not been altered.

8
00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:33.659
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Cryptographic binding uses hash functions to create that verifiable link. It ensures that if any part of the bound data changes, the binding breaks, and the change becomes detectable.

9
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.880
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): To detect changes, you need to know what to expect. You must find the original digest of the original binding.

10
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:54.290
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Then you can calculate the digest of the content you have and compare. If your calculated digest doesn't match the expected value, you know something has been changed. In decentralized identity systems, cryptographic binding is often used to support immutability and traceability.

11
00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:01.850
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): For example, the first record of a DID document contains information about an identity, its public keys, service endpoints, and other metadata.

12
00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.409
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): If we apply a cryptographic hash function to that record, we generate a digest.

13
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:13.810
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That digest is a unique fingerprint of the content. Even a tiny change to the document would produce a completely different digest.

14
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:31.219
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): By doing this, we have bound the contents of the DID document together. If someone modifies the record later, a referenced digest will no longer match the actual content digest. The mismatch proves that the content has changed. Some DID methods take this further by using the digest itself as part of the DID identifier.

15
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:45.079
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In those cases, the DID becomes self-certifying. The identifier directly verifies the integrity of its initial record. Binding can also continue over time. Suppose the identity rotates its keys and publishes a new version of its DID document.

16
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.870
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): While the DID specification itself does not require cryptographic chaining between versions, some DID methods implement it.

17
00:02:51.900 --> 00:03:00.149
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In methods that use hash-change storage, such as didWebVH and didWebS, the new record includes the digest, the previous record.

18
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:14.170
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This creates a verifiable sequence where each update is cryptographically linked to the one before it. Now this identity history becomes tamper evident. If someone attempts to rewrite an earlier record, every subsequent digest in the chain would fail to verify.

19
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:18.220
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The binding does not prevent change, but it makes change detectable.

20
00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:30.749
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That distinction is important. Cryptographic binding does not freeze data in place. Instead, it creates integrity guarantees. It allows identities to evolve, rotating keys, updating endpoints, adding services.

21
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:39.239
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): while preserving a trustworthy, verifiable history. It provides the mathematical glue that links identifiers, keys, and records together across time.

22
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:46.370
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Without binding, decentralized identifiers would be mutable documents. With binding, they become auditable identity systems.

